A British Columbia health team says that one cultural-awareness workshop proves its service is culturally safe for First Nations patients. What does that claim overlook?

31 March 2023

One workshop in an office does not make a health service culturally safe for First Nations patients. This is one of the findings in a report by the Office of the Health and Safety Officer. The report examines what happened when a First Nations man was injured in a car accident outside of Vancouver. He was taken to an office in Vancouver, where he was assessed by a health care team. The team included a nurse, a paramedic, and a physiotherapist. The team did not consider or recognize the man’s cultural identity or the trauma he had experienced. The man did not receive the care he needed. The report says that culturally safe care is about more than just having a cultural-awareness workshop. It is about having a team that understands, respects, and responds to the unique needs and experiences of Indigenous patients. The report also identifies some of the systemic barriers that can prevent Indigenous patients from accessing culturally safe care. These barriers include racism, lack of cultural understanding, and inadequate training and resources. The report makes several recommendations to improve the quality and safety of care for Indigenous patients. These include:

- Developing and implementing policies and practices that promote cultural safety and respect for Indigenous patients;

- Providing ongoing education and training for health care providers on cultural safety and Indigenous health;

- Ensuring that health care providers have the necessary resources and support to provide culturally safe care;

- Engaging with Indigenous communities and organizations to understand their needs and perspectives.

The report concludes that culturally safe care is essential for the health and well-being of Indigenous patients. It is not just a matter of having a cultural-awareness workshop, but of having a team that understands and respects the unique needs and experiences of Indigenous patients.

